#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110263 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b903c40f-a2e5-5084-9c10-b2d33f423f1e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Mean"}] :question/id #uuid "9662033b-2a3a-5d80-91ad-ed542898b80b" :question/math-academy-id "e-14544" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X \\sim N(\\mu, 6^2)$ with $P(X > 4) = 0.1841$, find $\\mu$.\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.00 \\,$ | $\\, 0.01 \\,$ | $\\, 0.02 \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\, 0.7 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7580 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7611 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7642 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.8 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7881 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7910 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7939 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.9 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8159 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8186 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8212 \\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the given probability into a cumulative probability using the complement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X \\le 4) &= 1 - P(X > 4) \\\\\n&= 1 - 0.1841 \\\\\n&= 0.8159\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X \\le 4) &= 0.8159 \\\\\nP(Z \\le \\frac{4 - μ}{6}) &= 0.8159 \\\\\nΦ(\\frac{4 - μ}{6}) &= 0.8159 \\\\\n\\frac{4 - μ}{6} &= Φ^{-1}(0.8159)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\Phi(0.90) = 0.8159 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\Phi^{-1}(0.8159) = 0.90\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{4 - μ}{6} &= 0.90 \\\\\n4 - μ &= 5.4 \\\\\nμ &= -1.4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110264 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "765f5ee4-b683-5c14-8e99-32241c029f39" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Variance or Standard Deviation"}] :question/id #uuid "35cc4c4c-79fb-5ae2-94e0-79f007af832b" :question/math-academy-id "e-14545" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X\\sim N(2.5, \\sigma^2)$ and $P(X \\geq 3) = 0.4013$, find $\\sigma^2$.\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.03 \\,$ | $\\, 0.04 \\,$ | $\\, 0.05 \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\, 0.2 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5910 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5948 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5987 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.3 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6293 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6331 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6368 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.4 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6664 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6700 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6736 \\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the given probability into a cumulative probability using the complement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X \\le 3) &= 1 - P(X \\ge 3) \\\\\n&= 1 - 0.4013 \\\\\n&= 0.5987\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X \\le 3) &= 0.5987 \\\\\nP(Z \\le \\frac{3 - 2.5}{σ}) &= 0.5987 \\\\\nΦ(\\frac{0.5}{σ}) &= 0.5987 \\\\\n\\frac{0.5}{σ} &= Φ^{-1}(0.5987)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\Phi(0.25) = 0.5987 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\Phi^{-1}(0.5987) = 0.25\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{0.5}{σ} &= 0.25 \\\\\nσ &= \\frac{0.5}{0.25} \\\\\nσ &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nσ^{2} = 2^{2} = 4\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110265 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d2bd470f-5459-5860-94cd-4ce9e6b70642" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Missing Parameter Using Symmetry When the Mean Is Zero"}] :question/id #uuid "2a8633b4-f937-57e3-899d-c3176f07a48f" :question/math-academy-id "e-14546" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X \\sim N(0, \\sigma^2)$ is a normal random variable with $P(-3.18 < X < 3.18) = 0.4038$, find $\\sigma$.\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.02 \\,$ | $\\, 0.03 \\,$ | $\\, 0.04 \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\, 0.4 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6628 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6664 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6700 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.5 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6985 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7019 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7054 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.6 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7324 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7357 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7389 \\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the cumulative probability using symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(-3.18 < X < 3.18) &= 0.4038 \\\\\n2P(0 < X < 3.18) &= 0.4038 \\\\\nP(0 < X < 3.18) &= 0.2019 \\\\\nP(X < 3.18) - P(X < 0) &= 0.2019 \\\\\nP(X < 3.18) - 0.5 &= 0.2019 \\\\\nP(X < 3.18) &= 0.7019 \\\\\nP(X \\le 3.18) &= 0.7019\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X \\le 3.18) &= 0.7019 \\\\\nP(Z \\le \\frac{3.18 - 0}{σ}) &= 0.7019 \\\\\nΦ(\\frac{3.18}{σ}) &= 0.7019 \\\\\n\\frac{3.18}{σ} &= Φ^{-1}(0.7019)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\Phi(0.53) = 0.7019 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\Phi^{-1}(0.7019) = 0.53\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3.18}{σ} &= 0.53 \\\\\nσ &= \\frac{3.18}{0.53} \\\\\nσ &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110266 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "45c41284-7cb9-5581-9b8f-5bbfce5aad0c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Missing Parameter Using Symmetry When the Mean Is Nonzero"}] :question/id #uuid "5eb435ff-ef12-5f18-89d6-4ac163fae344" :question/math-academy-id "e-14548" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X \\sim N(\\mu, 2^2)$ is a normal random variable with $P(-4\\mu< X < 6\\mu) = 0.6528$, find $\\mu$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.03 \\,$ | $\\, 0.04 \\,$ | $\\, 0.05 \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\, 0.7 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7673 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7704 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7734 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.8 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7967 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7995 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8023 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.9 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8238 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8264 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8289 \\,$ |\n\n*Hint: Note that the interval $-4\\mu< X < 6\\mu$ is symmetric about the mean $\\mu$.*" :question/worked-solution "Note that we can write the given probability as\n\n$$\nP(\\mu -5\\mu < X < \\mu +5\\mu) = 0.6528\n$$\n\nwhich tells us that the interval $-4\\mu< X < 6\\mu$ is symmetric about the mean $\\mu$.\n\nFirst, we find the cumulative probability using symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(μ - 5μ < X < μ + 5μ) &= 0.6528 \\\\\n2P(μ < X < μ + 5μ) &= 0.6528 \\\\\nP(μ < X < μ + 5μ) &= 0.3264 \\\\\nP(X < μ + 5μ) - P(X < μ) &= 0.3264 \\\\\nP(X < μ + 5μ) - 0.5 &= 0.3264 \\\\\nP(X < μ + 5μ) &= 0.8264 \\\\\nP(X \\le 6μ) &= 0.8264\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X \\le 6μ) &= 0.8264 \\\\\nP(Z \\le \\frac{6μ - μ}{2}) &= 0.8264 \\\\\nΦ(\\frac{5μ}{2}) &= 0.8264 \\\\\n\\frac{5μ}{2} &= Φ^{-1}(0.8264)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\Phi(0.94) = 0.8264 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\Phi^{-1}(0.8264) = 0.94\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{5μ}{2} &= 0.94 \\\\\n5μ &= 1.88 \\\\\n&= μ \\mid \\approx 0.38\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111350 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d2bd470f-5459-5860-94cd-4ce9e6b70642" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Missing Parameter Using Symmetry When the Mean Is Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab90957b-206e-541c-b3e0-40deacd9bc4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "121" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186110196} {:answer/id #uuid "b18d11f8-2b80-50a8-98b2-4d634e744f04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "144" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186110197} {:answer/id #uuid "c35148cc-b8e6-5ac7-aac6-e81e8071c142" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "81" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186110198} {:answer/id #uuid "20c2b68e-e133-5da1-a51f-514b1f9985b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "169" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186110199} {:answer/id #uuid "59d47685-43c2-55b1-a86a-71905719c776" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186110200}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b18d11f8-2b80-50a8-98b2-4d634e744f04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "144" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186110197} :answer-field/id #uuid "15701758-eeb3-5aac-812f-2fe665fc4e8b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186110195}] :question/id #uuid "0de7ef2c-c461-55a3-92ec-92284bc49b94" :question/math-academy-id "q-94892" :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nThe data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(0, σ^{2})$ is a normal random variable with $P(\\mid X \\mid < 9) = 0.5468$, find $σ^{2}$.\n\n| $z$ | $0.04$ | $0.05$ | $0.06$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0.7$ | $0.7704$ | $0.7734$ | $0.7764$ |\n| $0.8$ | $0.7995$ | $0.8023$ | $0.8051$ |\n| $0.9$ | $0.8264$ | $0.8289$ | $0.8315$ |" :question/requires-calculator true}]}
